1. pydantic/networks.py:465 — `pretty_email_regex = re.compile(r'([\w ]*?) *<(.*)> *')` used with `.fullmatch(value)` at networks.py:482 in `validate_email`. Nested quantifier with overlapping character classes (`[\w ]*?` adjacent to literal space ` `, where `\w` matches no space but the ambiguity plus the trailing/leading space handling and `.*` tail) allows catastrophic backtracking on attacker-supplied `EmailStr`/`NameEmail` input — the CVE-2024-3772 pattern. Reachable unauthenticated via any API request body/query param validated with `EmailStr`/`NameEmail`.

2. pydantic/datetime_parse.py:34 (standard_duration_re, lines 34-42) — `^(?:(?P<days>-?\d+) (days?, )?)?((?:(?P<hours>-?\d+):)(?=\d+:\d+))?(?:(?P<minutes>-?\d+):)?(?P<seconds>-?\d+)(?:\.(?P<microseconds>\d{1,6})\d{0,6})?$`. Multiple optional groups each beginning with `-?\d+` and separated by `:` / space produce exponential ambiguity on long digit strings terminated by a non-matching char (e.g. "1:1:1:1:...:1x"). Applied to attacker strings in `parse_duration` (datetime_parse.py:224). This is the CVE-2021-29510 core pattern.

3. pydantic/datetime_parse.py:45 (iso8601_duration_re, lines 45-55) — `^(?P<sign>[-+]?)P(?:(?P<days>\d+(.\d+)?)D)?(?:T(?:(?P<hours>\d+(.\d+)?)H)?(?:(?P<minutes>\d+(.\d+)?)M)?(?:(?P<seconds>\d+(.\d+)?)S)?)?$` — repeated `\d+(.\d+)?` sub-patterns with an unescaped `.` (dot also matching digits) create nested ambiguity per component plus a chain of optional `T...H...M...S` groups; long digit strings that fail final anchoring cause super-linear/exponential backtracking. Also part of CVE-2021-29510 and reachable via `parse_duration` on user data (pydantic Timedelta / duration validators).

Assessed but not submitted separately (lower confidence / derived from same literals): date_expr/time_expr (lines 23-27) and the parse_datetime/parse_time match sites (lines 118, 150, 188, 224) reuse those same compiled patterns and are input-independent in structure — the exploitable backtracking lives in the duration regexes already reported. Similarly the networks.py URL/domain regexes (lines 68-81, 90-92, 101) did not show a clear catastrophic-backtracking path: `[^\s/:?#]+` and the `(?:chunk\.)*?chunk` alternative boundaries are character-disjoint enough, and the maximal request-length cap (`max_length = 2**16`) is bounded; those were treated as not-confirmed rather than submitted.

Severity: high (unauthenticated, cheap single-request CPU exhaustion in any service validating EmailStr/NameEmail/duration fields with pydantic 1.8), CWEs cwe-1333 and cwe-400.
